+# Spec (addendum) — Mizaneh round 2: fundamental upgrade
+
+Builds on `.scratch/mizaneh-audit/spec.md` (round 1). See `CONTEXT.md` §9
+for why each item was scoped the way it was, and why ingredient-level
+gram quantities were explicitly rejected.
+
+## User Stories
+
+23. As a user, I want the shopping list to show how many meals this week
+    need each ingredient, so I have a real sense of how much of it I'll
+    use without the app pretending to know an exact purchase weight it
+    doesn't have.
+24. As a user, I want to see a few alternatives when I substitute a meal,
+    not just one automatic swap, so I can pick the one I actually want.
+25. As a user, I want the option to save my current plan on this device,
+    so I don't have to redo the calculator every time I open the app.
+26. As a returning user with a previously saved plan, I want to be asked
+    whether to restore or discard it — never have it silently reappear —
+    so I stay in control of my own data.
+27. As a privacy-conscious user, I want the app's own privacy statement to
+    accurately describe when data is and isn't stored, even after the
+    save feature ships, so the statement never becomes misleading.
+28. As a user, I want a "پاک کردن داده‌های ذخیره‌شده" control that fully
+    removes anything saved on this device, so I can undo the choice to
+    save at any time.
+29. As a user on a phone, I want to be able to install Mizaneh to my home
+    screen and keep using it without a network connection, so a slow or
+    absent connection doesn't block a tool that never needed the network
+    in the first place.
+
+## Implementation Decisions
+
+- **Shopping list (`lib/plan/shopping.ts`)**: `buildShoppingGroups` is
+  extended so each group's items carry a count of how many meals in the
+  week reference that ingredient string; `buildShoppingList`'s `note`
+  copy is updated to explain the count. The old `ShoppingGroup.items:
+  string[]` shape stays available for anything relying on the plain list;
+  a new `ShoppingGroup.entries: { name: string; count: number }[]` is
+  added for the count-aware UI, so nothing that reads `.items` breaks.
+- **Substitution picker (`lib/plan/build.ts`)**: new
+  `getSubstituteOptions(day, slot, occurrence, form, variationSeed, limit
+  = 3)` returns the top-N ranked alternatives (same `candidateMeals` +
+  `scoreMeal` used by `buildWeeklyPlan`/`substituteMeal`) without
+  mutating anything; `substituteMeal` itself is kept (now implemented as
+  "apply the first option from `getSubstituteOptions`") so existing round
+  1 tests/behavior are unaffected. A new `components/SubstitutePicker.tsx`
+  lists the options with a "select" action per option.
+- **Session persistence (`lib/storage/session.ts`, new)**: a small,
+  pure-ish module wrapping `localStorage` behind `saveSession`,
+  `loadSession`, `clearSession`, gated entirely behind explicit calls from
+  the UI (never called on mount without a user action triggering it,
+  except the one-time "do you want to restore?" check, which reads but
+  does not apply anything automatically). Stored payload is versioned
+  (`{ version: 1, form, planMode, variationSeed, substitutions }`) so a
+  future shape change can detect and discard incompatible old data instead
+  of crashing.
+- **Safety/privacy copy**: `components/SafetyAdvisory.tsx` copy updated;
+  new `components/SaveSessionControls.tsx` (save / restore-prompt / clear)
+  gets its own explicit, accessible copy — never bundled silently into
+  an existing control.
+- **PWA**: `public/sw.js` (new, minimal cache-first service worker for the
+  static shell) + registration in `app/layout.tsx`, guarded by
+  `if ("serviceWorker" in navigator)` and only in the browser (not during
+  SSR/build). `app/manifest.ts` reviewed and left as-is unless a concrete
+  defect is found.
+
+## Testing Decisions
+
+- Shopping-list count logic: unit tests on `buildShoppingGroups`/
+  `buildShoppingList` for count correctness and the updated note text.
+- `getSubstituteOptions`: unit tests for option count, uniqueness,
+  compatibility (same constraints as round 1's `substituteMeal` tests),
+  and that `substituteMeal` still behaves identically to round 1 (regression).
+- Session persistence: unit tests against a mocked `localStorage cover
+  save/load/clear/version-mismatch-discard, entirely at the `lib/storage`
+  level (no DOM needed).
+- Component tests: saving requires an explicit click (no write on mount
+  or on plan generation alone); a returning session triggers a visible,
+  dismissible prompt rather than silently repopulating the form; clearing
+  removes the stored key.
+- PWA: a lightweight test that the service worker file exists and is
+  valid JS (parses), plus a build-level check that registration code is
+  guarded correctly (no `window`/`navigator` access during the Next.js
+  server build). Full offline-behavior testing is out of scope for this
+  environment (would need a real browser/service-worker test harness);
+  logged as a follow-up.
+
+## Out of Scope (round 2)
+
+- Ingredient-level gram quantities (rejected, see CONTEXT.md §9.1).
+- Any server sync of a saved session (stays device-local, by design).
+- Push notifications (no backend to send them from).
