+
+# Spec: Mizaneh production-readiness audit & refactor
+
+## Problem Statement
+
+Mizaneh works today but lives entirely in one 2677-line component file that
+mixes UI, nutrition math, a hardcoded food catalogue, and plan-building
+logic. From the user's perspective this shows up as: unclear or missing
+guidance when they enter something odd (e.g. a very low age or an
+unreachable target weight), no visible statement that the tool isn't
+medical advice, a shopping list that only lists items rather than combining
+quantities, and — invisibly to them but risky for the product — no tests
+protecting the calorie/macro formulas, the deterministic plan generator, or
+the "nothing is ever sent anywhere" privacy promise from silent regressions.
+
+## Solution
+
+Split the single file into cohesive modules (formulas, food data, plan
+generation, state, UI), each independently testable; add the safety
+advisory and improved validation/accessibility identified in the prototype
+evaluation; make the shopping list state clearly that it groups rather than
+sums quantities (until ADR-0002's follow-up); and cover all of the above
+with a real automated test suite, without changing any formula constant,
+dataset value, or the no-network/no-storage privacy behavior.
+
+## User Stories
+
+1. As a user, I want to see a clear one-time note that Mizaneh is an
+   estimate and not medical advice, so that I understand the tool's limits
+   before I rely on its numbers.
+2. As a user, I want my age/height/weight/target-weight inputs validated
+   with a specific, understandable message when they're out of a sane
+   range, so that I know exactly what to fix.
+3. As a user with an input outside the formulas' well-validated range
+   (e.g. age under 18, estimated BMI under 16), I want a visible, non-blocking
+   advisory suggesting professional guidance, so that I'm informed without
+   being blocked from seeing my estimate.
+4. As a user, I want my calorie/macro targets computed with the same
+   Mifflin–St Jeor formula, activity multipliers, deficit cap, and calorie
+   floors the app already uses, so that my numbers don't silently change
+   between visits or releases.
+5. As a user with a food allergy, I want every meal in my plan to exclude
+   my selected allergens, so that I never see a plan I can't safely eat.
+6. As a user with disliked foods listed, I want those foods excluded from
+   my plan, so that I get a plan I'll actually follow.
+7. As a user choosing a diet style (vegetarian / mediterranean / high
+   protein / balanced), I want my plan's macro targets and meal choices to
+   reflect that style, so that the plan matches how I actually want to eat.
+8. As a user, I want to regenerate variety for my plan and get a different
+   but equally valid arrangement, so that I'm not stuck with one option.
+9. As a user, I want regenerating with the same inputs and the same
+   variation seed to reproduce the exact same plan, so that a shared plan
+   (e.g. a screenshot or a saved link) is trustworthy and reproducible.
+10. As a user, I want to substitute one planned meal for another compatible
+    one (same slot, same allergy/dislike/style constraints), so that I can
+    adjust the plan to my taste without breaking my calorie targets.
+11. As a user, I want my week's shopping list grouped and de-duplicated
+    across meals, and clearly labeled as a grouped list rather than exact
+    purchase quantities, so I'm not misled about precision it doesn't have.
+12. As a user navigating the 4-step form by keyboard only, I want every
+    control reachable and operable via keyboard with a visible focus state,
+    so that I can complete the calculator without a mouse.
+13. As a screen-reader user, I want every field, error, and the safety
+    advisory to have correct accessible labels/roles, so that I can use
+    Mizaneh with assistive technology.
+14. As a mobile user, I want the RTL layout, form controls, and results
+    tables to be usable and legible on a small screen, so that I can use
+    Mizaneh on my phone (the primary expected device).
+15. As a user with reduced-motion preferences set, I want animations
+    suppressed or minimized, so that the app doesn't trigger discomfort.
+16. As a user, I want to print or export my plan as text, so that I can
+    keep a copy off-screen (e.g. on my fridge or in a notebook).
+17. As a privacy-conscious user, I want confidence that my form data is
+    never sent over the network or written to persistent browser storage,
+    so that the "nothing leaves your browser" promise is actually true.
+18. As the site owner, I want correct metadata, structured data (JSON-LD),
+    sitemap, and robots output preserved after the refactor, so that SEO
+    and link-preview behavior don't regress.
+19. As a maintainer, I want nutrition formulas, unit conversions, and
+    rounding rules to live in exactly one module, so that there is a single
+    source of truth instead of scattered constants.
+20. As a maintainer, I want the food catalogue (meal templates) separated
+    from UI code, so that adding or editing meals doesn't require touching
+    component logic.
+21. As a maintainer, I want the deterministic plan-ranking function
+    (`hashValue` + scoring) extracted into a pure, unit-testable module, so
+    that regressions in plan generation are caught before release.
+22. As a maintainer, I want a CI-runnable test suite (`npm run test`)
+    covering the categories listed in "Testing Decisions" below, so that
+    `lint`/`test`/`build`/`audit` gate every future change.
+
+## Implementation Decisions
+
+- **New module boundaries** (all under `app/` or a new `lib/` sibling,
+  following existing Next.js app-router conventions):
+  - `lib/nutrition/formulas.ts` — BMR/TDEE/deficit/macro/BMI/fiber/water
+    calculations (pure functions, no React, no formatting).
+  - `lib/nutrition/format.ts` — Persian number/decimal formatting, digit
+    conversion, unit rounding (`roundTo`) — the single source of truth for
+    display formatting.
+  - `lib/food/catalogue.ts` — the `MealTemplate` list and its types, with
+    no UI or planning logic.
+  - `lib/plan/build.ts` — `buildWeeklyPlan`, `hashValue`, slot-share and
+    scoring logic, substitution lookup — pure functions taking form +
+    metrics + mode + seed, returning `DayPlan[]`.
+  - `lib/plan/shopping.ts` — shopping-list grouping/aggregation from a
+    built plan.
+  - `app/DietPlanner.tsx` — becomes UI-only: form state, step navigation,
+    validation messages, the safety-advisory component, and rendering of
+    results produced by the `lib/*` modules.
+  - `components/SafetyAdvisory.tsx` — the trust-and-safety-first notice
+    selected in the prototype evaluation; shown once, with `role="note"`,
+    reused for the out-of-range per-field advisory from ADR-0001.
+- **Validation**: field-level validation functions live alongside
+  `lib/nutrition/formulas.ts` (pure, so testable without rendering the
+  component); the UI layer only wires messages to fields.
+- **Determinism**: `variationSeed` stays an explicit parameter threaded
+  from UI state into `lib/plan/build.ts`; no hidden global/module-level
+  mutable state in the extracted module.
+- **Substitution**: implemented in `lib/plan/build.ts` as a pure function
+  `substituteMeal(day, slot, occurrence, form, metrics)` returning a new
+  `DayPlan`, reusing the existing candidate-filtering/ranking logic.
+- **Shopping list**: `lib/plan/shopping.ts` groups by a normalized
+  ingredient key (trimmed, case-folded) and returns `{ items, note }` where
+  `note` states the list is grouped, not quantity-exact.
+- **Metadata/SEO**: `app/page.tsx`, `app/manifest.ts`, `app/robots.ts`,
+  `app/sitemap.ts` are reviewed for correctness but not restructured beyond
+  fixing any concrete defects found during `improve-codebase-architecture`.
+- No change to any numeric constant identified in `docs/research.md`.
+
+## Testing Decisions
+
+- Test public behavior (function inputs/outputs, rendered DOM/roles), not
+  internals — per the workflow's own instruction.
+- Prefer one seam per concern: `lib/nutrition/formulas.ts` and
+  `lib/plan/build.ts` get direct unit tests; `DietPlanner.tsx` gets
+  component/interaction tests (step navigation, validation, keyboard nav,
+  a11y roles) via Testing Library; no test suite exists yet, so the
+  smallest suitable stack is added (Vitest + @testing-library/react +
+  jsdom), matching Next.js/React 19 without adding an unnecessary second
+  runner.
+- Categories to cover (mapped to modules):
+  - Mifflin–St Jeor / TDEE / deficit-safeguard / floor / rounding →
+    `lib/nutrition/formulas.test.ts`.
+  - Invalid & extreme inputs (negative age, non-numeric, absurd height) →
+    same file, plus `DietPlanner` validation-message tests.
+  - Persian number formatting → `lib/nutrition/format.test.ts`.
+  - Dietary style / allergy / dislike filtering, deterministic seeded
+    generation, substitution, day-pattern snack bias → `lib/plan/build.test.ts`.
+  - Shopping-list grouping → `lib/plan/shopping.test.ts`.
+  - Step navigation, field validation UX, keyboard nav, RTL/mobile
+    rendering smoke tests, safety-advisory presence/roles → `DietPlanner.test.tsx`.
+  - Privacy/no-persistence: a test asserting no `fetch`/`XMLHttpRequest`/
+    `localStorage`/`sessionStorage` call occurs during a full form
+    submission (spy-based).
+  - Print/text export: a test asserting the export output contains the
+    expected plan content.
+  - Metadata/build: covered by the required `npm run build` validation
+    step rather than a unit test (Next.js metadata objects are largely
+    declarative).
+
+## Out of Scope
+
+- Ingredient-level substitution and quantity-exact shopping lists (ADR-0002
+  follow-up).
+- Any backend, account system, or data persistence — Mizaneh stays
+  client-only by design.
+- Switching to a dynamic (differential-equation) weight-loss model —
+  the static estimate is kept per `docs/research.md`, with a disclosure
+  instead of a formula change.
+- Localization into languages other than Persian.
+- The three-way `?variant=` prototype route itself (deleted after this
+  spec captured its decision).
+
+## Further Notes
+
+- All calculation constants are treated as frozen per this audit's
+  guardrail ("do not silently change formulas, datasets, ... or
+  scientific claims"); every constant referenced above is verified, not
+  altered, in `docs/research.md`.
+- `docs/adr/0001-*.md` and `docs/adr/0002-*.md` record the two decisions
+  substantial enough to need one.
